    "result_text": "The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers.\n\n**What the spec required:** reject with 400 when a telephone's national-number length is wrong for its country code \u2014 `+61` requires 9 national digits, `+1` requires 10.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New `CountryCallingCode` enum** (`util/CountryCallingCode.java`) \u2014 a small, single-responsibility unit that knows the recognised E.164 calling codes and each one's required national-digit count (`AUSTRALIA` \u2192 `61`/9, `NANP` \u2192 `1`/10). It exposes `forDigits(...)` (longest-prefix match to identify the country) and `hasValidNationalLength(...)`. Adding future countries is a one-line enum constant.\n\n2. **`TelephoneNormalizer.toE164(...)`** \u2014 after the existing digit-count/all-digits checks, it now looks up the calling code and, when recognised, rejects (returns `Optional.empty()`) if the national length doesn't match. Unrecognised codes keep the existing generic 8\u201315 total-digit rule, so no other country's behaviour changes.\n\nBecause the normalizer is the shared source of truth used by both the `@TelephoneDigits` create-time constraint and the `OwnerMapper` storage mapping, the new rule applies consistently \u2014 the constraint returns 400 at the boundary, exactly as the acceptance test verifies.\n\nThe reject test (`\"+61 123\"` \u2192 3 national digits \u2260 9 \u2192 400) passes, and the existing valid-owner flows still pass since the `04XXXXXXXX` generator yields exactly 9 national digits under `+61`. Full suite: **1 passing, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS.**",
